  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin with precip from Tropical Storm Isaias \nmoving northward into central GA. By daybreak Fri the storm is \nexpected to be a hurricane and centered due south of Mobile Bay \nacross the central Gulf. There is still some uncertainty with the \nintensity, track, and timing of the storm but things appear to be \ncoming together for this system to make landfall near the AL/FL \nborder between 4z and 8z Sat morning as a Cat 1 hurricane. The \ncurrent forecast track shows Isaias moving Northward through AL Sat \nwith its remnant moving up into KY and the Mid-Atlantic area through \nSunday. As this system moves onshore the models have been very \nconsistent showing the high pressure ridge over the Mid-Atlantic \nstates setting up as a wedge down the eastern seaboard and into NE \nGA. This ridge/wedge will act as a steering mechanism and help to \nkeep the center of Isaias to our west. This will put GA on the more \nactive side of the storm with increased winds and rain expected \nacross the state. Current QPF showing 1 to 3 inch totals across the \nstate Fri night through Sun morning. Winds will begin ramping up Fri \nnight with sustained winds in he 10-15mph range by daybreak Sat. \nSustained winds will continue to increase into the 15-20mph range \nthrough Sat afternoon with gust up to 40mph. The strongest winds \nwill be across North and western portions of the state as the \npressure gradient tightens between the ridge/wedge to the NE and \nIsaias moving north through AL. Looks like wind speeds peak Sat \nafternoon/evening then trail off sat night as the system weakens.\n\nBy Sun afternoon High pressure begins building back into the area as \na developing ridge over East TX becomes centered along the MS/AL \ngulf coast. The exiting tropical system also begins to bring in \nsome drier air from this ridge clearing things out across GA \nthrough Sun night. By Mon morning this ridge is expected to be \ncentered over central AL and should keep things warm and dry.",
